"Places discrete event marks on parallel entity rows to reveal timing, bursts, and synchronization.\n\nUse when: Events have timestamps and belong to clearly identified subjects or sources. Timing matters more than a smoothed event rate.\n\nAvoid when: The event volume is too dense for individual marks to remain visible.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes event time. Row identifies the entity; each equal-sized tick encodes one event occurrence.\n\nBuild: Define entities and a common observation window. Draw one short tick per timestamp in the corresponding row. Label rows and disclose unavailable observation periods separately from no events.\n\nCheck: Check duplicate timestamps represent real repeated events. Verify all rows share the same time zone and units. Confirm time without monitoring is distinguishable from monitored time without events.\n\nPitfalls: Tick length should not imply event duration unless duration is actually encoded. Overplotting can hide bursts; use a binned time heatmap when individual ticks merge.\n\nExample: Show synthetic machine alert occurrences across twelve devices during one hour, with an event tick on each device row.\n\nAlternatives: Calendar heatmap, Lasagna plot, Histogram."
